#{[{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116137} :activity/id #uuid "37fc70ba-9218-5dd8-9943-c92ffa172cf7" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116137 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116499 :tutorial/content "Just like for $2\\times 2$ matrices, there are also **elementary $3\\times 3$ matrices**. These are matrices that can be obtained by applying *only one* elementary row operation to the identity matrix ![](images/52/5242669707ba340c10907369ae842f98d630582a9e96c9b562eec4e6671d4c6c.svg)\n\nFor instance, consider the following $3\\times 3$ matrices:\n\n![](images/f6/f6a165394b479865919c1a3eff8541ec424fd42e3c487df3d3592f664fe8715c.svg)\n\nThese are all examples of elementary matrices:\n\n- $E_1$ is obtained from $I_3$ by applying the row operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2.$\n\n- $E_2$ is obtained from $I_3$ by applying the row operation $R_2 := 2R_2.$\n\n- $E_3$ is obtained from $I_3$ by applying the row operation $R_2 := R_2 + 3R_1.$\n\nThey are also examples of the three different types of elementary matrices, defined by the corresponding row operations." :tutorial/id #uuid "4d18540e-8992-5cf5-9984-837579edd2a3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3168 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "22f135ef-2bf7-53c7-bbbd-7ceb73df118d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116138}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116138 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115252 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0303bd06-294e-5f7a-bbd2-b12abdeb93c5"} :step/id #uuid "80e2ff0b-2184-50d7-9e9f-70274a3019ad" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116140}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116139 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115253 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41954d0a-b8ce-52a0-a2c0-002b0078a37d"} :step/id #uuid "8f9639d5-a5fb-5b6c-8aca-eb75b34a00ab"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116140 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116500 :tutorial/content "As we saw before for elementary $2\\times 2$ matrices, each elementary $3\\times 3$ matrix $E$ is invertible, and the corresponding inverse is always an elementary matrix of the same type as $E.$ To illustrate, let's consider the matrix \n\n![](images/a1/a1c71fda15d78cc8c6ae95862c95b73a1cca1830cdf3b54f8e7e151653b82df1.svg)\n\nTo find the inverse of $E,$ we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1:** Write down $\\color{blue}E$ and the identity matrix $I_3$ side-by-side, as shown below:\n![](images/1e/1ef9574c45fb103c7fa2b32af13018de53da495fdf9e6e2da729897a521cead9.svg)\n\n**Step 2:** Apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix. In our case, we need to apply the transformation $R_2 := R_2 + (-4)R_1.$ We apply the operation to entire row (not only the left part):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,{I}_{3}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[{I}_{3}\\,|\\,{E}^{-1}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $R_2 := R_2 + (-4)R_1$ transforms $E$ back into the identity matrix. Likewise, when this row operation is applied to the identity matrix, it generates $E^{-1}.$\n\nThe matrix $E^{-1}$ is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore,\n![](images/fa/fa0e6c42fc8efaf80c3a37640261b1ec8247c2d598f14d7af4865a7c57691654.svg)\n\nWe can work out the inverse of all elementary $3\\times 3$ matrices in a similar way." :tutorial/id #uuid "90445bd9-d2af-593e-a10d-9e7c94156e49" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3169 :tutorial/title "The Inverse of an Elementary Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "e927ae81-0817-515b-8603-18914b2317b0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116141}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116141 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115254 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd71abb1-455e-50d0-aae4-082b41821ef1"} :step/id #uuid "0ad61d7d-e259-50fa-af0b-671e9d0310ef" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116139}}] :activity/title "Elementary 3x3 Matrices" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115477}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186048897 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115252 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115081 :question/id #uuid "083bea4b-54a3-52c9-a1a6-512a9c33d86f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4726" :question/problem "Is \n![](images/90/90028e6f3030f9e5b93718e55a0eeb4e01e5d0b09540f38ceda9718058791603.svg)\nan elementary matrix?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that an elementary matrix is obtained by performing exactly one elementary row operation on the identity matrix $I.$\n\nThe given matrix $E$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I$ by applying the elementary row operation $R_2 := R_2 + 8R_1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+8{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0303bd06-294e-5f7a-bbd2-b12abdeb93c5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115859 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9216142-01de-5e59-af76-ee91754c2843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114092} {:answer/id #uuid "03ff09ea-c004-5da8-8d98-ee6d1f3b9cee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114093} {:answer/id #uuid "5e105675-5508-52e1-9277-ae5f3dfa891c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$, $B$, and $C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114094} {:answer/id #uuid "74bed431-6f45-53fc-ba09-92492b47ca53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114095} {:answer/id #uuid "74c90517-71bc-5073-81c4-a9b1e0d3098d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114096}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d7b78288-309b-59b9-b202-87737f9fccf6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114091}] :question/id #uuid "7b4f2457-18b6-549a-b3ed-0be0c1710764" :question/math-academy-id "q-43504" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}, C = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115861 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98c4c87f-09c7-5e9a-b6a5-029734245381" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477325} {:answer/id #uuid "44f62344-515d-5274-9c6e-607d5a5b1e56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477326}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477325} :answer-field/id #uuid "c18ab024-61b0-55dd-8cd5-3b5d03d5bd7f" :answer-field/key "P" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477632} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79ebd9f8-93bd-5df1-9313-eab2b5635228" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477327} {:answer/id #uuid "379250a1-a18a-5cea-80a6-b6640f3f7561" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477328} :answer-field/id #uuid "645a4e8e-b0ab-5b4d-afb5-d13f5affc3a7" :answer-field/key "Q" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477633} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93929904-e248-512d-967a-b6030ad20a3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477329} {:answer/id #uuid "571eddfb-765e-505f-8b07-eee9c08d2c85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477330}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477330} :answer-field/id #uuid "771dc8c0-f925-55a9-9eea-e2734e1718f4" :answer-field/key "S" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477634}] :question/id #uuid "fb7edba7-7f03-5a91-ad50-58a8e20f5eb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-43509" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,S=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that an elementary matrix is obtained by performing exactly one elementary row operation on the identity matrix $I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $P$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from $I$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $Q$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:=7{R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=7{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =Q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $S$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+7{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =S\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $Q$ and $S$ are elementary matrices."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247104 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "055fd93a-dc3c-5837-bb7e-b10d2b5f586e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461611} {:answer/id #uuid "a80c3c5e-d9b4-5318-b1f6-d20a3da16b9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461612}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461611} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2ae2459-61e0-5b34-a36e-84d3e8989367" :answer-field/key "A" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462559} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff5be9c2-3b47-5c46-95d5-045358b54872" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461613} {:answer/id #uuid "47ec3cb1-9a8d-5ae1-8405-a39633aac020" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461614} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4ff86b1-edc5-53ff-a7db-1086daba7c30" :answer-field/key "B" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462560} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "630bb051-3efe-5a74-a086-01a568b3085a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461615} {:answer/id #uuid "c689ac4f-c64b-54da-8d4d-e1500110c4ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461615} :answer-field/id #uuid "3af09c95-73be-5007-8368-88b917550558" :answer-field/key "C" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462561}] :question/id #uuid "11f8af48-def1-5000-8ab6-3793fdd8fd3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-110291" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that an elementary matrix is obtained by performing exactly one elementary row operation on the identity matrix $I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from $I$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $B$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from $I$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\nTherefore, only $B$ is an elementary matrix."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247105 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75fb586e-f579-51cf-bd9d-47ff1486b729" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461617} {:answer/id #uuid "bdc11fdc-ce52-5c36-bad6-aca6df0112c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461617} :answer-field/id #uuid "57d8c5f5-4748-5d5e-b3fc-406fcb8ac660" :answer-field/key "A" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462562} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "201ce91a-f2af-5228-b077-e9c4dec01ceb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461619} {:answer/id #uuid "b6d897c7-655f-57c2-810f-6244cedbf59b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461620}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461620} :answer-field/id #uuid "74e56853-cb78-585c-8cc8-8e2d2f6a89ae" :answer-field/key "B" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462563} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae49a308-7222-54d7-88af-541e4b74369b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461621} {:answer/id #uuid "f95ae002-6924-5fc8-bdc2-e3df6ecce540" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461622} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfb38ab3-fc30-5c9a-b73e-48670489fbfe" :answer-field/key "C" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462564}] :question/id #uuid "a6d84ae8-2da9-539e-89fc-1e2b037b4578" :question/math-academy-id "q-110296" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that an elementary matrix is obtained by performing exactly one elementary row operation on the identity matrix $I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from $I$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $B$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1}↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:=(-5){R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $B$ and $C$ are elementary matrices."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247106 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a20caaf5-55ed-50a8-9c16-3db9d13f58f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461623} {:answer/id #uuid "88a54ef8-32b5-5135-87f6-e5f1333a4f81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461624} :answer-field/id #uuid "54012b06-a154-5d01-a786-1580b0324414" :answer-field/key "A" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462565} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96e31214-3d9d-56fa-8a9e-04bc79bd837a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461625} {:answer/id #uuid "ed987963-27f0-581d-9e0a-71c1f56eff2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461626} :answer-field/id #uuid "d239a3f5-a495-5008-bcae-4c74c9d8e615" :answer-field/key "B" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462566} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f62530ad-31bd-5684-97fa-aa4e1c0e075e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461627} {:answer/id #uuid "fadb0d1e-0584-5d37-83f4-5d3313790b10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461628} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e637bd1-fb81-53d1-ad8f-f5e388adf393" :answer-field/key "C" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462567}] :question/id #uuid "a29a5217-fb22-5ba1-a37e-09a1f7203c3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-110297" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that an elementary matrix is obtained by performing exactly one elementary row operation on the identity matrix $I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+9{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+9{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}↔{R}_{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+4{R}_{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+4{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ are elementary matrices."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399692 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b645e6f3-cd35-5190-89ea-905a43b97f3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470865} {:answer/id #uuid "3b58b46a-21b6-513d-85af-11c3fc78f339" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470866}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470866} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d8f1b12-d894-535d-837c-b4623e346301" :answer-field/key "A" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471938} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed486786-83e5-5b10-a764-fef9951fd846" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470867} {:answer/id #uuid "c9c594b0-0ea2-5fec-9ad1-311266b85329" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470868}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470868} :answer-field/id #uuid "913b6bcd-d9a0-5ed1-83a8-a626e0572b57" :answer-field/key "B" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.typ