wer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553005} {:answer/id #uuid "53d86092-7471-55f7-9d63-a98a063e4774" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553048} {:answer/id #uuid "591a54ba-9e63-5a4f-b824-96016e64e338" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553060} {:answer/id #uuid "6fc43cba-98c4-5316-9315-73ee5dc7178c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553095} {:answer/id #uuid "7b207b0f-ff4b-5106-b545-34950451ba86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553095} :answer-field/id #uuid "c45807ed-0b5d-5ba7-9a08-20c241cb211c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553467}] :question/id #uuid "0d572b3b-a41f-5942-974d-aa05a4f6167a" :question/math-academy-id "q-43503" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices? \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that an elementary matrix is obtained by performing exactly one elementary row operation on the identity matrix $I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices. \n\n- The matrix $A$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from $I$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $B$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{3},$ as   follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $B$ and $C$ are elementary matrices."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553497 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "155d715a-6591-5f0a-a5d1-3230104e5c0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552906} {:answer/id #uuid "31974ed2-8ab2-5860-9969-3c8d2557d61d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552961} {:answer/id #uuid "3677e299-6a46-53d9-923e-d66455517593" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552972} {:answer/id #uuid "6e6c7555-a521-5ba2-8b3b-ea8f17016137" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553093} {:answer/id #uuid "95cbf98e-49b4-5964-8fa9-ba9edae9dace" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553173} :answer-field/id #uuid "96673328-15bd-5e46-aed8-a10911f9f6d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553447}] :question/id #uuid "0e6443e6-00e6-5ef0-aee1-9ffa4b651465" :question/math-academy-id "q-110295" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that an elementary matrix is obtained by performing exactly one elementary row operation on the identity matrix $I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from $I$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $C$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $A$ and $C$ are elementary matrices."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553545 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29fa9687-52b3-5782-ab16-13bebbd62113" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552945} {:answer/id #uuid "4d3f13a9-dfae-5d12-adbb-d151ad4bfadb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553037} {:answer/id #uuid "4eabbab2-598e-58aa-88f5-c96f3a31432e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553041} {:answer/id #uuid "c2960d98-aee3-5881-bf5f-70e5d585b629" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553255} {:answer/id #uuid "cc064898-ea25-5c51-9b7c-dd26a1d65b7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553276}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553276} :answer-field/id #uuid "56dfb04b-3d87-515f-b3a5-a58dfa625373" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553415}] :question/id #uuid "85c5bb7a-1f82-53c7-81de-6727d67bcbab" :question/math-academy-id "q-110290" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that an elementary matrix is obtained by performing exactly one elementary row operation on the identity matrix $I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from $I$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from $I$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\nTherefore, only $A$ is an elementary matrix."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553579 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08a090c4-f656-58fd-865f-15e47f6dea20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552878} {:answer/id #uuid "61759cc0-91ac-561c-a3d9-afca8ae82ece" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553076} {:answer/id #uuid "6fd7ec47-6f68-5146-9884-0bc2068559a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553098} {:answer/id #uuid "cb6b5b51-0b84-5583-a3de-1767e5591ad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$, $B$, and $C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553275} {:answer/id #uuid "f84e15f9-7b9e-5694-aaf9-af7395576f4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553361}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$, $B$, and $C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553275} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4e0c779-554a-50a7-861e-ad750e8a4f93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553454}] :question/id #uuid "ddd0989b-5b62-525c-8707-9699dca205bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-43506" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that an elementary matrix is obtained by performing exactly one elementary row operation on the identity matrix $I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-10){R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-10){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{3}:=7{R}_{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=7{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-9){R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-9){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$, $B$, and $C$ are elementary matrices."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553585 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d1dd63e-83ad-53b0-9275-276b52fce777" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$, $B$, and $C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552952} {:answer/id #uuid "4a4d03b3-bf03-53f9-800e-6e3153bebf0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553026} {:answer/id #uuid "520ef72a-b53e-5c61-92fe-f73fc93906ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553046} {:answer/id #uuid "7eeaeecd-58c3-5752-8535-c7daae2e602d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553124} {:answer/id #uuid "d59d61d0-4a12-57b9-8e72-ec80f4d7a8eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553298}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553046} :answer-field/id #uuid "492c6565-bbc7-58b8-b8e3-1a4c7fbf73b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553410}] :question/id #uuid "ebbbdfa0-a865-5ee5-9dbc-03f4d6b092c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-43510" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that an elementary matrix is obtained by performing exactly one elementary row operation on the identity matrix $I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices. \n\n- The matrix $A$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:=-8{R}_{1},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=-8{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from $I$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $C$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+10{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+10{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $A$ and $C$ are elementary matrices."}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Elementary 3x3 Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115253 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115082 :question/id #uuid "18e5d6e0-a024-503b-87f5-b20080980b51" :question/math-academy-id "e-4727" :question/problem "Find the product $E_{1}^{-1}E_2$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -11 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{E}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $E_1$ and $E_2$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find $E_1^{-1}.$ We write down $E_1$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{1}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -11 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+11{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 11 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n![](images/76/763f9f0cad2acdece8b1b45a3fd129f653ca3f9f17b1f63580bf64d32fa1228d.svg)\n\nNow, we can find the product $E_1^{-1} E_{2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/d9/d9239c7c15d5f5cb0fabaecceef33e2a1b7797b4a9a05134d328c5f3ad89cb33.svg)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41954d0a-b8ce-52a0-a2c0-002b0078a37d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115746 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13b3e217-78fc-5209-bb0d-5db7fb853808" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552902} {:answer/id #uuid "7f2c8067-385e-5090-ac90-4d243a57c60e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -11 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553126} {:answer/id #uuid "ca0892a3-5a8f-5836-acc6-aa516cb4df4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553271} {:answer/id #uuid "d6822e4a-83af-5313-9cbb-35c28a1d50a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553299} {:answer/id #uuid "e0bb4433-b5c9-57d6-88a2-3aa4713cf503" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552902} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f5426f9-9f4f-5bd4-9358-fb1b1872bdb9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113442}] :question/id #uuid "31e37627-dbcf-5042-bfd2-a4b02810031e" :question/math-academy-id "q-32127" :question/problem "Find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{2}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{2}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{2}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{E}_{2}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -11\\end{aligned}]\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115862 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e2699c1-732f-5da5-8aef-3986c96f29d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114110} {:answer/id #uuid "afe9a300-9870-5857-8a91-842f1e315ea5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114111} {:answer/id #uuid "d4b787ce-47cc-5372-9a39-2e372872f9bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114112} {:answer/id #uuid "436e59a3-933f-582b-9855-6fc637e5e296" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114113} {:answer/id #uuid "4b6a2eed-1908-58da-b151-8c1e402b5667" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 9 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114114}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c7837c0c-1f42-5e84-97b7-d5997bfd75ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114109} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1ae786d-9920-51fb-8732-75b94263fe92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392549} {:answer/id #uuid "c6bc1964-42df-5092-acce-5b935b9482bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :an