er the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{E}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345042 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13f63618-8846-5383-aae6-4dbaaf8b19f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342413} {:answer/id #uuid "3e30f118-5a78-53e9-a1ef-b9644cb5a0ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342750} {:answer/id #uuid "41c335d3-ab39-5b8b-859c-8b87612e9ca3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342777} {:answer/id #uuid "47e207db-d162-5b2a-aabc-04c06199aec4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342832} {:answer/id #uuid "9b36de3a-b505-5603-aad8-74e749f4e5f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342750} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c55726b-9c40-57f3-8435-1d4f7d77ea3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344619}] :question/id #uuid "0854eeb0-38e4-505f-a728-28edf544308c" :question/math-academy-id "q-75192" :question/problem "What is the inverse of the elementary matrix \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $B$ and the identity matrix $I$ si