 ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${A}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266082 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01d17947-1f1d-52bd-8878-b1b300a0284c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263015} {:answer/id #uuid "0e3903bb-a5a5-55c7-b7b5-37235b09fab7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263129} {:answer/id #uuid "7a976fe2-255e-5036-852f-baa69838e34e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264063} {:answer/id #uuid "acc9a313-7778-5547-93b5-d97d3ed2aac2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264486} {:answer/id #uuid "c43d018e-0191-500d-8cad-039a446e5d9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264486} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d0a2b95-1c44-5a0e-9ffd-e044b5511da5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265269}] :question/id #uuid "5f07f03a-e06a-534c-b617-9ac5ff79722f" :question/math-academy-id "q-131381" :question/problem "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${A}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 &  &  \\\\ 0 & 1 &  & \\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $A$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${A}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266083 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b128fa8-6be9-58a8-b4d3-3beeda3f6639" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{E}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264327} {:answer/id #uuid "a82e2ab7-ebf9-5891-9632-6768fc20891f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{E}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264437} {:answer/id #uuid "ebb2255e-4ef1-5533-8df5-2545ba54b270" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{E}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265060} {:answer/id #uuid "ebb86a64-235b-57b4-8b20-a23658e17ebe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{E}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265061} {:answer/id #uuid "ff20709c-64e9-52d5-bd00-7155481f15d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{E}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{E}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264437} :answer-field/id #uuid "44fca2f4-7c56-5f34-b083-0f5dadcb0dac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265376}] :question/id #uuid "89d7bb5d-6c75-5357-9f2d-e9d22519ad6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-131384" :question/problem "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $E$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${E}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 &  &  \\\\ 0 & 1 &  & \\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $E$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $E$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${E}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{E}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299700 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a987cf7-3d87-5d25-bd5c-dd880b1daa91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297782} {:answer/id #uuid "96bd62c8-0cca-5580-ae04-416b82048473" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298206} {:answer/id #uuid "bf3c5dd7-085b-57d7-9575-a9f3f0bf6441" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298392} {:answer/id #uuid "c817c443-b57e-5f24-8265-b649a1f3ac77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298426} {:answer/id #uuid "d1bc9edf-5bdb-5311-bb9b-39a82ba608ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{B}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298392} :answer-field/id #uuid "a66018ec-422c-5e0e-b376-357aef68a84e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299198}] :question/id #uuid "d5898f79-8d87-5e96-a0ad-da8516834a84" :question/math-academy-id "q-31871" :question/problem "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $B$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${B}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[B\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}} & \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{\\phantom{a}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $B$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $B$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[B\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${B}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{B}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329703 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cb923d4-b5d1-551e-bd0c-32922527b318" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{C}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}15 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327043} {:answer/id #uuid "71821817-c757-531b-9efc-24a73efee7a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{C}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}15 & -2 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327912} {:answer/id #uuid "80c4ef71-2974-507c-8dc4-cedd29fab1e6" :answer/type {: